Gateinto for Linux (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)

Not yet verified on a real Linux desktop. Linux has no device key yet, so
Gateinto connects to gateways without a device certificate.

The gateinto executable needs glibc 2.38 or newer. That was measured from the
glibc symbol versions of a release build made on Ubuntu 24.04; it has not
been run on other distributions or older glibc versions.

Verify the tarball, extract it into a new, empty directory and install
from there, for the current user, not as root and not through sudo:

    sha256sum -c gateinto-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz.sha256 &&
      mkdir gateinto-tarball &&
      tar -xzf gateinto-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz -C gateinto-tarball --strip-components=1 &&
      cd gateinto-tarball &&
      ./install.sh

The commands stop at the first failure, so nothing is extracted or run when
the checksum does not match or gateinto-tarball already exists.

install.sh copies gateinto to ~/.local/bin/gateinto (creating ~/.local/bin
if needed), replaces an earlier copy there, runs
`~/.local/bin/gateinto uri register` to register the gateinto:// handler,
and warns when ~/.local/bin is not on PATH. It creates no device key and
writes no configuration.

Optional systemd user unit: gateinto-agent.service runs
`~/.local/bin/gateinto agent` while a graphical session is running
(graphical-session.target). install.sh does not install it. To enable it,
from gateinto-tarball:

    mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user &&
      cp gateinto-agent.service ~/.config/systemd/user/ &&
      systemctl --user enable --now gateinto-agent

Only one agent runs per user. If one started on demand is already
running, run `gateinto stop` first (it ends that agent's sessions and
their browsers); otherwise the unit's agent fails, is retried until the
start limit, and the unit stays failed until it is started again, for
example at the next login, although `enable --now` reported success.

Desktops that do not start graphical-session.target never start it.
Stopping the unit ends the browsers the agent started, as `gateinto stop`
does. After installing a newer gateinto, run
`systemctl --user restart gateinto-agent`. To disable it without
uninstalling Gateinto:

    systemctl --user disable --now gateinto-agent &&
      rm ~/.config/systemd/user/gateinto-agent.service

Uninstall, from the directory that holds gateinto-tarball:

    cd gateinto-tarball && ./uninstall.sh

If ~/.config/systemd/user/gateinto-agent.service exists and systemctl is on
PATH, uninstall.sh first runs
`systemctl --user disable --now gateinto-agent.service`. It then runs
`~/.local/bin/gateinto stop --remove-stale-profiles`,
which stops a running agent with its sessions and deletes the Gateinto
browser profiles left behind, then `~/.local/bin/gateinto uri unregister`,
then removes the unit file (followed by `systemctl --user daemon-reload`)
and ~/.local/bin/gateinto. If disabling, stopping or unregistering fails,
it stops and keeps the binary, so running it again retries; a failed
daemon-reload only prints a warning. It keeps the configuration and the
gateways added from links in ~/.config/gateinto (or
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gateinto).

With the unit installed, run uninstall.sh from your own login session:
under `su -`, `sudo -u` or without a user manager,
`systemctl --user disable --now` fails to reach your user manager (the
enablement link may already be gone) and uninstall.sh stops. If the unit
file is a symbolic link to a missing file (as `systemctl --user link`
leaves after the linked file is deleted), uninstall.sh changes nothing
and asks you to run `systemctl --user stop gateinto-agent` if it is
running, to remove by hand that link and, if present,
~/.config/systemd/user/graphical-session.target.wants/gateinto-agent.service,
and to run it again.

